{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'rg -n -C 3 \"title|Table.grid|grid\" docs/source/tables.rst docs/source/reference/table.rst'","aggregated_output":"docs/source/tables.rst-10-    from rich.console import Console\ndocs/source/tables.rst-11-    from rich.table import Table\ndocs/source/tables.rst-12-\ndocs/source/tables.rst:13:    table = Table(title=\"Star Wars Movies\")\ndocs/source/tables.rst-14-\ndocs/source/tables.rst-15-    table.add_column(\"Released\", justify=\"right\", style=\"cyan\", no_wrap=True)\ndocs/source/tables.rst-16-    table.add_column(\"Title\", style=\"magenta\")\n--\ndocs/source/tables.rst-50-\ndocs/source/tables.rst-51-There are a number of keyword arguments on the Table constructor you can use to define how a table should look.\ndocs/source/tables.rst-52-\ndocs/source/tables.rst:53:- ``title`` Sets the title of the table (text shown above the table).\ndocs/source/tables.rst-54-- ``caption`` Sets the table caption (text shown below the table).\ndocs/source/tables.rst-55-- ``width`` Sets the desired width of the table (disables automatic width calculation).\ndocs/source/tables.rst-56-- ``min_width`` Sets a minimum width for the table.\ndocs/source/tables.rst:57:- ``box`` Sets one of the :ref:`appendix_box` styles for the table grid, or ``None`` for no grid.\ndocs/source/tables.rst-58-- ``safe_box`` Set to ``True`` to force the table to generate ASCII characters rather than unicode.\ndocs/source/tables.rst-59-- ``padding`` An integer, or tuple of 1, 2, or 4 values to set the padding on cells (see :ref:`Padding`).\ndocs/source/tables.rst-60-- ``collapse_padding`` If True the padding of neighboring cells will be merged.\n--\ndocs/source/tables.rst-70-- ``header_style`` Set the default style for the header.\ndocs/source/tables.rst-71-- ``footer_style`` Set the default style for the footer.\ndocs/source/tables.rst-72-- ``border_style`` Set a style for border characters.\ndocs/source/tables.rst:73:- ``title_style`` Set a style for the title.\ndocs/source/tables.rst-74-- ``caption_style`` Set a style for the caption.\ndocs/source/tables.rst:75:- ``title_justify`` Set the title justify method (\"left\", \"right\", \"center\", or \"full\")\ndocs/source/tables.rst-76-- ``caption_justify`` Set the caption justify method (\"left\", \"right\", \"center\", or \"full\")\ndocs/source/tables.rst-77-- ``highlight`` Set to True to enable automatic highlighting of cell contents.\ndocs/source/tables.rst-78-\n--\ndocs/source/tables.rst-82-You can set the border style by importing one of the preset :class:`~rich.box.Box` objects and setting the ``box`` argument in the table constructor. Here's an example that modifies the look of the Star Wars table::\ndocs/source/tables.rst-83-\ndocs/source/tables.rst-84-    from rich import box\ndocs/source/tables.rst:85:    table = Table(title=\"Star Wars Movies\", box=box.MINIMAL_DOUBLE_HEAD)\ndocs/source/tables.rst-86-\ndocs/source/tables.rst-87-See :ref:`appendix_box` for other box styles.\ndocs/source/tables.rst-88-\n--\ndocs/source/tables.rst-115-\ndocs/source/tables.rst-116-You may also add columns by specifying them in the positional arguments of the :class:`~rich.table.Table` constructor. For example, we could construct a table with three columns like this::\ndocs/source/tables.rst-117-\ndocs/source/tables.rst:118:    table = Table(\"Released\", \"Title\", \"Box Office\", title=\"Star Wars Movies\")\ndocs/source/tables.rst-119-\ndocs/source/tables.rst-120-This allows you to specify the text of the column only. If you want to set other attributes, such as width and style, you can add a :class:`~rich.table.Column` class. Here's an example::\ndocs/source/tables.rst-121-\n--\ndocs/source/tables.rst-124-        \"Released\",\ndocs/source/tables.rst-125-        \"Title\",\ndocs/source/tables.rst-126-        Column(header=\"Box Office\", justify=\"right\"),\ndocs/source/tables.rst:127:        title=\"Star Wars Movies\"\ndocs/source/tables.rst-128-    )\ndocs/source/tables.rst-129-\ndocs/source/tables.rst-130-Column Options\n--\ndocs/source/tables.rst-155-Grids\ndocs/source/tables.rst-156-~~~~~\ndocs/source/tables.rst-157-\ndocs/source/tables.rst:158:The Table class can also make a great layout tool. If you disable headers and borders you can use it to position content within the terminal. The alternative constructor :meth:`~rich.table.Table.grid` can create such a table for you.\ndocs/source/tables.rst-159-\ndocs/source/tables.rst-160-For instance, the following code displays two pieces of text aligned to both the left and right edges of the terminal on a single line::\ndocs/source/tables.rst-161-\n--\ndocs/source/tables.rst-163-    from rich import print\ndocs/source/tables.rst-164-    from rich.table import Table\ndocs/source/tables.rst-165-\ndocs/source/tables.rst:166:    grid = Table.grid(expand=True)\ndocs/source/tables.rst:167:    grid.add_column()\ndocs/source/tables.rst:168:    grid.add_column(justify=\"right\")\ndocs/source/tables.rst:169:    grid.add_row(\"Raising shields\", \"[bold magenta]COMPLETED [green]:heavy_check_mark:\")\ndocs/source/tables.rst-170-\ndocs/source/tables.rst:171:    print(grid)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
